      <div class='static-page__title-row'>
        <h1 class='static-page__title--sinai'>Syriac Parchment Manuscripts Description Project</h1>
      </div>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          The Syriac Parchment Manuscripts Description Project was the first phase in an effort to provide comprehensive scholarly description for the Arabic and Syriac manuscripts of St. Catherine's Monastery of the Sinai. Undertaken as part of the Sinai Manuscripts Digital Library (SMDL), the project set out to expand access to and discovery within the Monastery's Syriac parchment manuscripts — including the small but significant number written in Christian Palestinian Aramaic — through systematic codicological description and work-level identification of their texts.
        </p>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
          Carried out by an interdisciplinary team of specialist scholars in Syriac and Christian Arabic manuscript studies, the project produced descriptions for 114 Syriac parchment manuscripts, resulting in 81 corrected work identifications and 199 newly identified texts now discoverable through SMDL's descriptive metadata. The project also produced 305 colophon transcriptions and translations, documenting the names, dates, and places attested within them. Beyond the Syriac-language content, the project documented and described Arabic content present within the manuscripts, such as marginal notes, accompanying texts, bilingual passages, and liturgical rubrics.
        </p>
      </div>

      <hr class='divider'>

      <div class='static-page__row'>
        <h3 class='static-page__subtitle static-page__subtitle--sinai'>Funding and Sponsorship</h3>
        <p class='static-page__text static-page__text--sinai'>
        The project received generous financial support from the National Endowment for the Humanities, through a Humanities Collections and Reference Resources grant ("Identification and Description of the Syriac and Arabic Manuscripts at St. Catherine's Monastery of the Sinai, Part 1: Syriac Parchment Manuscripts"), and from the Steinmetz Foundation.
        </p>
      </div>

